(899159, 101817, 93, '<td valign="top">:) Sorry, my original comment on this was a little lacking :)<br/> <br/>I still do find this shot very crazy. Whoever stacked that wood... haha..  <br/> <br/>Anyway, congrats on a good finish.. 34th out of 300 photographs is quite an achievement!  I thought this was a very clever take on the challenge, and scored it a 7 :)  I thought the color was a bit off (a bit too far in the yellow), and I was wondering if an element in the window wouldn''t have helped some (some kind of light maybe.. a ghost of a face?) <br/>Just some things to think about.<br/>Good going :)</td>', ':) Sorry, my original comment on this was a little lacking :) I still do find this shot very crazy. Whoever stacked that wood... haha..   Anyway, congrats on a good finish.. 34th out of 300 photographs is quite an achievement!  I thought this was a very clever take on the challenge, and scored it a 7 :)  I thought the color was a bit off (a bit too far in the yellow), and I was wondering if an element in the window wouldn''t have helped some (some kind of light maybe.. a ghost of a face?) Just some things to think about.Good going :)', '2004-09-08 04:35:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(899229, 100397, 16907, '<td valign="top">350 votes?! I''m amazed... Thank you for your votes and comments!<br/> <br/>I must say - I''ve had lower expectations for this one. Why? Well, there is a little story behind it and maybe some of you might find it interesting or useful.<br/>This particular picture is in fact a recreation of one of my earlier photos. The original photo was taken about 6 months ago, won a few contests and was proudly retired. Those contests had nothing to do with framing or any other theme, just an ordinary POTD contests.<br/>Then, I saw ''Framing'' contest on DPC... I remembered the retired photo and decided to repeat the shooting session, not only to follow the DPC rules, but to find out what has changed in 6 months regarding my shooting skills and such... The important thing was not to look at the original photo because that would have killed the experiment. However, as I had found out later, there was a downside to this - two pictures were not taken at the same time of the day (nor the same time of the year for that matter).<br/>So... went on the location, took a few shots, picked one picture, cropped it, adjusted levels and shadow/highlight ratio, played with curves, resized the edited picture and here we are.<br/>Any improvement in 6 months? That''s for you to judge, here''s the older <a href="//www.shutterbugs.biz/cgi-bin/display.pl?potd_date=May%209th,%202004&amp;artist=Ivica%20Bozanic&amp;src=''//www.shutterbugs.biz/potdentries/winners/14119.jpg''&amp;title=''Framed''" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">picture</a>.</td>', '350 votes?! I''m amazed... Thank you for your votes and comments! I must say - I''ve had lower expectations for this one. Why? Well, there is a little story behind it and maybe some of you might find it interesting or useful.This particular picture is in fact a recreation of one of my earlier photos. The original photo was taken about 6 months ago, won a few contests and was proudly retired. Those contests had nothing to do with framing or any other theme, just an ordinary POTD contests.Then, I saw ''Framing'' contest on DPC... I remembered the retired photo and decided to repeat the shooting session, not only to follow the DPC rules, but to find out what has changed in 6 months regarding my shooting skills and such... The important thing was not to look at the original photo because that would have killed the experiment. However, as I had found out later, there was a downside to this - two pictures were not taken at the same time of the day (nor the same time of the year for that matter).So... went on the location, took a few shots, picked one picture, cropped it, adjusted levels and shadow/highlight ratio, played with curves, resized the edited picture and here we are.Any improvement in 6 months? That''s for you to judge, here''s the older picture.', '2004-09-08 05:01:45.000000', NULL, 0),
